```c
#include <stdint.h>

union SensorData {
    struct {
        uint16_t force;     // 16-bit force
        uint32_t adc1;      // 32-bit ADC reading
        uint32_t adc2;      // 32-bit ADC reading
    } fields;
    uint8_t raw[sizeof(uint16_t) + 2 * sizeof(uint32_t)];  // raw data
};
```

In this union, `SensorData` can be accessed either as individual fields (`force`, `adc1`, `adc2`) or as a raw byte array (`raw`). The `sizeof` function is used to ensure that the raw data array is large enough to hold all of the fields.

Please note that the order of fields in a struct can affect the layout in memory due to something called padding. This is done to optimize access to the fields on some systems. If you need a specific layout in memory, you may need to look into packed structs, but be aware that this can affect performance and is not always portable.

Also, the `stdint.h` header is used to ensure that the data types have a specific size (16 bits for `force` and 32 bits for `adc1` and `adc2`). This header is not available in all C environments, but it is part of the C99 standard and is widely supported.

