To create a Chrome extension that can track all audio sources in the browser, you'll need to follow these steps. Please note that this extension will only have access to audio sources from web pages that the user visits, and it won't be able to track audio from local files or other applications.

1. Create a new folder named "audio-tracker" to store all the necessary files for your Chrome extension.

2. Inside the "audio-tracker" folder, create a new file named "manifest.json". This file is responsible for providing important information about your extension to Chrome. Add the following content to your "manifest.json" file:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Audio Tracker",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Track all audio sources in the browser.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "declarativeContent", "scripting"],
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icon16.png",
      "48": "icon48.png",
      "128": "icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["contentScript.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```

3. Create a new file called "contentScript.js" inside the "audio-tracker" folder. This script will be injected into the webpages the user visits to keep track of audio sources:

```javascript
function handleAudioElement(audioElement) {
  const audioSource = audioElement.src;
  const event = new CustomEvent("audioSourceDetected", {
    detail: {
      audioSource
    }
  });

  // Dispatch the event to be caught in the popup.js
  window.dispatchEvent(event);
}

// Loop through existing audio elements
const audioElements = document.getElementsByTagName("audio");
for (let i = 0; i < audioElements.length; i++) {
  handleAudioElement(audioElements[i]);
}

// Listen for new audio elements
const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
  mutations.forEach(mutation => {
    Array.from(mutation.addedNodes)
      .filter(node => node.tagName === 'AUDIO')
      .forEach(handleAudioElement);
  });
});

observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });
```

4. Create a "popup.html" file inside the "audio-tracker" folder. This file will represent the popup UI of your Chrome extension:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Audio Tracker</title>
  <style>
    body {
      width: 300px;
      height: 400px;
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    }
    ul {
      list-style-type: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      overflow: auto;
      height: 86%;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Audio Sources:</h1>
  <ul id="audioSourcesList"></ul>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

5. Create a "popup.js" file inside the "audio-tracker" folder. This file will handle the communication between the content script and the popup:

```javascript
function addAudioSourceToList(audioSource) {
  const list = document.getElementById("audioSourcesList");
  const listItem = document.createElement("li");
  listItem.textContent = audioSource;
  list.appendChild(listItem);
}

window.addEventListener("audioSourceDetected", (e) => {
  addAudioSourceToList(e.detail.audioSource);
});
```

6. To complete your Chrome extension, prepare the icons in the sizes specified in the manifest.json file (icon16.png, icon48.png, and icon128.png) and place them in the "audio-tracker" folder.

7. Open Google Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions/`. Enable "Developer mode" at the top right corner. Click on "Load unpacked" and select the "audio-tracker" folder to load your extension.

You now have an audio tracker Chrome extension that can track all the audio sources in the browser. Keep in mind that this simple implementation has room for improvement, like removing duplicate audio sources and handling various edge cases. Good luck!

Sure, I can help you write a JavaScript function that calculates the relativistic distance to an object. However, it's important to note that in special relativity, the concept of distance becomes more complex due to length contraction and the intermingling of space and time in the form of spacetime.

In the simplest case, if we're considering an object moving away from us at a constant velocity, we can calculate the distance using the formula:

`distance = velocity * time`

However, if we want to take into account relativistic effects, we would need to know the velocity of the object as a significant fraction of the speed of light. In that case, we can use the formula for time dilation to calculate the distance as perceived by a stationary observer:

`distance = (velocity / sqrt(1 - (velocity^2 / speed_of_light^2))) * time`

Here's a JavaScript function that implements this formula:

```javascript
function calculateRelativisticDistance(velocity, time) {
    const speedOfLight = 299792458; // meters per second

    // Prevent division by zero or imaginary numbers
    if (velocity >= speedOfLight) {
        return "Velocity cannot be equal to or greater than the speed of light.";
    }

    const distance = (velocity * time) / Math.sqrt(1 - (Math.pow(velocity, 2) / Math.pow(speedOfLight, 2)));

    return distance;
}
```

This function takes in the velocity (in meters per second) and time (in seconds), and returns the distance (in meters). Please note that this function does not take into account the effects of acceleration, gravity, or other forces, which could significantly affect the calculation in a real-world scenario.

Also, remember that JavaScript might not be the best language for precise scientific calculations due to its floating-point number representation. For high-precision calculations, consider using a language more suited to this task, such as Python with appropriate libraries.

Yes, it is possible to use Tailwind CSS in a way that's similar to Styled Components. This can be achieved by using a library called "twin.macro", which allows you to use Tailwind classes within your styled components. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to set this up:

1. First, make sure you have both Tailwind CSS and Styled Components installed in your project:

```bash
npm install tailwindcss styled-components
```

2. Install the required dependencies for twin.macro:

```bash
npm install twin.macro @emotion/react @emotion/styled
```

3. Create or update your Tailwind CSS configuration file (`tailwind.config.js`). If you haven't already set up Tailwind in your project, follow the [official Tailwind CSS installation guide](https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation).

4. Configure twin.macro in your project by creating a `babel-plugin-macro.config.js` file in your project root with the following content:

```js
module.exports = {
  twin: {
    preset: 'styled-components',
    styled: {
      import: 'default',
      from: 'styled-components/macro',
    },
    autoCssProp: true,
  },
};
```

5. Now, you can use Tailwind CSS classes within your styled components like this:

```jsx
import tw from 'twin.macro';
import styled from 'styled-components';

const Button = styled.button`
  ${tw`bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded`}
`;

function App() {
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <Button>Click me</Button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

With this setup, you can take advantage of both Tailwind CSS's utility classes and Styled Components' component-specific styling. Make sure to check the [official twin.macro documentation](https://github.com/ben-rogerson/twin.macro) for more information and advanced usage.

